1
00:00:01,150 --> 00:00:07,321
SAMson extracts the brain from rodent MRI images with the Segment Anything Model, SAM.

2
00:00:07,291 --> 00:00:14,469
This tutorial shows its app step by step, on a T2-weighted mouse image: the example of the SAMson notebook.

3
00:00:15,769 --> 00:00:22,845
Once the package is installed, and SAM's model downloaded, the app starts from a terminal with the command samson app.

4
00:00:22,815 --> 00:00:28,248
Here we give it the folder with our image, and open the address it prints in a web browser.

5
00:00:29,948 --> 00:00:31,914
Step 1: Images.

6
00:00:32,714 --> 00:00:37,027
The app opens on the first of its five steps, listed in the sidebar.

7
00:00:37,439 --> 00:00:39,933
The folder we gave is already filled in.

8
00:00:39,903 --> 00:00:46,181
The file name filter can select some of its files, for example only the T2-weighted images.

9
00:00:46,151 --> 00:00:52,650
The table lists the images found, with their size, voxel size and the axis labels of their headers.

10
00:00:52,620 --> 00:00:57,863
Our image has 200 by 200 voxels and 18 coronal slices.

11
00:00:57,833 --> 00:01:04,467
The representative image is used to set up the whole dataset in the next steps. Here there is only one.

12
00:01:05,615 --> 00:01:12,995
The app also notices that the header stores the voxel sizes ten times too large, as some rodent datasets do.

13
00:01:12,965 --> 00:01:19,572
This does not change the masks; in step 3 we will set the scale, so that lengths are shown in millimetres.

14
00:01:19,542 --> 00:01:25,190
Other ways to start are the example mouse included with SAMson, and uploading an image.

15
00:01:25,160 --> 00:01:29,303
In the sidebar, the dataset specification records every choice.

16
00:01:29,273 --> 00:01:34,524
It can be downloaded as a file, loaded again later, or used from the command line.

17
00:01:38,182 --> 00:01:40,369
Step 2: Orientation.

18
00:01:41,469 --> 00:01:50,708
SAMson works on coronal slices with the dorsal side up, ordered from the caudal end, the cerebellum, to the rostral end, the olfactory bulbs.

19
00:01:50,678 --> 00:01:56,729
The figure shows our image as SAMson would read it, at the top, and SAMson's template, at the bottom.

20
00:01:56,699 --> 00:02:03,012
Our image covers only the middle of the brain, with 18 thick slices, so its side view is narrow.

21
00:02:02,982 --> 00:02:08,273
Below the options, the app shows the axis labels of the image header: R, S, P.

22
00:02:08,243 --> 00:02:14,121
The P means that the slices run from the front of the head to the back: the reverse of SAMson's order.

23
00:02:14,091 --> 00:02:17,866
The button “Guess from the image header” uses these labels.

24
00:02:18,090 --> 00:02:23,071
It ticks “Reverse anteroposterior axis”, and the setting now matches the header.

25
00:02:23,041 --> 00:02:29,727
The coronal slice stays upright, and in step 3 the coloured pairs will confirm the order of the slices.

26
00:02:29,697 --> 00:02:38,145
The same settings can be chosen by hand: the image axes that run from back to front and from bottom to top, and whether to reverse them.

27
00:02:38,115 --> 00:02:41,366
The caption gives the matching command-line options.

28
00:02:41,336 --> 00:02:48,404
The image is only re-oriented in memory: the masks are always saved on the grid of the original image.

29
00:02:51,113 --> 00:02:53,920
Step 3: Anteroposterior range.

30
00:02:55,020 --> 00:02:56,897
This is SAMson's key setting.

31
00:02:56,867 --> 00:03:02,741
For every slice, SAMson draws a box prompt: a rectangle that tells SAM where the brain is.

32
00:03:02,711 --> 00:03:11,672
Its shape comes from the matching slice of the template, so SAMson needs to know which template slices match the first and the last slices of our image.

33
00:03:11,642 --> 00:03:15,297
At the top are the side views of our image and of the template.

34
00:03:15,267 --> 00:03:20,218
Each coloured line in our image is matched to the line of the same colour in the template.

35
00:03:20,188 --> 00:03:29,870
The dashed line marks the anchor slice: there, SAMson finds the brain with an automatic threshold, median Otsu, and scales all the boxes from it.

36
00:03:29,840 --> 00:03:35,356
First, we set the header scale to 0.1, so that lengths are shown in millimetres.

37
00:03:35,326 --> 00:03:38,221
Now the app compares the lengths of both ranges.

38
00:03:38,191 --> 00:03:49,815
Our 18 slices span 8.5 millimetres, while the default template range, 4 to 33, spans 11.6, so the app warns that their ratio is unusual.

39
00:03:49,785 --> 00:03:52,233
The coloured pairs below show the problem.

40
00:03:52,203 --> 00:03:59,610
Our first slice is matched to template slice 4, which is only cerebellum, and our last slice to the very front of the brain.

41
00:03:59,580 --> 00:04:03,300
At both ends, the green boxes are too small for the brain.

42
00:04:03,270 --> 00:04:10,411
So we move the template range to 8 to 31, which is also the range used for this image in the SAMson notebook.

43
00:04:10,381 --> 00:04:17,561
The warning disappears: 8.5 millimetres against 9.2, a ratio of 0.92.

44
00:04:17,531 --> 00:04:23,281
Now each pair shows the same anatomy, and the green boxes surround the brain at both ends.

45
00:04:24,121 --> 00:04:32,764
Other options set the slices to process, link the lengths of the two ranges, move the side view, or change the median Otsu settings.

46
00:04:32,734 --> 00:04:37,563
With manual masks of a few subjects, the app can also suggest the range.

47
00:04:40,257 --> 00:04:42,155
Step 4: Segment.

48
00:04:43,256 --> 00:04:49,707
SAMson uses SAM's largest model, ViT-H, as in the article, on the GPU if there is one.

49
00:04:49,677 --> 00:04:54,702
The checkpoint, the file with the model's weights, was downloaded at installation.

50
00:04:54,672 --> 00:04:57,065
Full-Auto gives one mask per slice.

51
00:04:57,035 --> 00:05:03,948
Semi-Auto also keeps SAM's three candidate masks of every slice, so that we can choose the best one afterwards.

52
00:05:03,918 --> 00:05:05,644
We choose Semi-Auto.

53
00:05:05,614 --> 00:05:11,640
We can segment the representative image, all the images of the folder, or a selection of them.

54
00:05:11,610 --> 00:05:18,318
Then we choose the output folder, and whether to save quality-control figures and brain-extracted images.

55
00:05:18,288 --> 00:05:24,782
The app also writes the equivalent command, to process large datasets with exactly these settings.

56
00:05:24,752 --> 00:05:26,351
Let's run SAMson.

57
00:05:26,643 --> 00:05:28,692
SAM's model is loaded once.

58
00:05:28,662 --> 00:05:34,631
Then, for each slice, SAMson draws the box prompt, and SAM segments the brain inside it.

59
00:05:38,501 --> 00:05:45,418
Done: about ten seconds for the 18 slices on this GPU, and the masks are saved in the output folder.

60
00:05:48,104 --> 00:05:49,925
Step 5: Review.

61
00:05:51,025 --> 00:05:56,037
In Semi-Auto, each slice starts with its Full-Auto mask, marked with a tick.

62
00:05:56,007 --> 00:06:00,320
Next to it are SAM's three candidates, numbered 0, 1 and 2.

63
00:06:00,290 --> 00:06:05,918
“Zoom on the brain” shows only the region around it, and the slider moves through the slices.

64
00:06:07,286 --> 00:06:12,386
The montage shows the mask of every slice in blue, inside its green box prompt.

65
00:06:12,356 --> 00:06:15,027
The masks follow the outline of the brain.

66
00:06:14,997 --> 00:06:23,644
There is no manual mask for this mouse, so we check the result against the Full-Auto mask that the SAMson notebook produced for this image.

67
00:06:23,614 --> 00:06:32,169
The Jaccard index and the Dice coefficient are 1, which means perfect overlap: the two masks are identical, voxel by voxel.

68
00:06:32,139 --> 00:06:35,696
The app reproduces the method of the article exactly.

69
00:06:35,666 --> 00:06:45,864
With a manual mask as reference, this button picks the best candidate of every slice, as in the Semi-Auto evaluation of the article. Here we do not need it.

70
00:06:45,834 --> 00:06:50,607
In the notebook, the Semi-Auto mask used candidate 1 in every slice.

71
00:06:50,577 --> 00:06:54,955
We do the same: we set every slice to candidate 1, and apply.

72
00:06:55,757 --> 00:07:00,001
Now we compare it with the Semi-Auto mask that the notebook produced.

73
00:06:59,971 --> 00:07:02,517
Again, the two masks are identical.

74
00:07:02,487 --> 00:07:05,843
Semi-Auto also lets us correct single slices.

75
00:07:05,813 --> 00:07:12,871
On slice 1, candidate 1 has a notch on the right side, while the Full-Auto mask follows the edge of the brain.

76
00:07:12,841 --> 00:07:16,173
So, for this slice, we choose the Full-Auto mask.

77
00:07:16,214 --> 00:07:17,713
Finally, we save.

78
00:07:17,683 --> 00:07:26,666
SAMson writes the mask, on the grid of the original image, with the candidates, a record of every setting and choice, and a quality-control figure.

79
00:07:26,636 --> 00:07:30,132
Saved results can be reopened later from their folder.

80
00:07:32,486 --> 00:07:43,334
In summary: SAMson segmented this mouse brain in about ten seconds, and the app gives exactly the masks of the SAMson notebook, in Full-Auto and in Semi-Auto.

81
00:07:46,334 --> 00:07:51,642
Here is a closer look at six of the final slices, from the back of the brain to the front.

82
00:07:55,442 --> 00:08:02,008
For a whole dataset, the specification saved by the app is used with the command samson segment.

83
00:08:05,308 --> 00:08:09,359
The user guide explains every option. Thank you for watching.

